+\section{Skill Utility and Reuse on SkillsBench}
+\label{sec:skillsbench-experiments}
+
+We first ask whether completed skills help on their source tasks, then whether that utility extends when the tasks change. Repeated standard-task evaluations establish the observed benefit of deploying the final artifacts. Controlled variants examine their scope of reuse, and inspection of skill content and execution traces connects these outcomes to the decisions made during deployment.
+
+\begin{figure*}[t]
+\centering
+\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/variant-evaluation-logic.pdf}
+\caption{Evaluation of skill reuse under task variation. A source-task skill is frozen and reused under representation (P1), instance (P2), and requirement (P3) changes. Comparison with a no-skill execution of the same variant measures the completed artifact's utility.}
+\label{fig:variant-evaluation}
+\end{figure*}
+
+\subsection{Experimental Setup}
+
+We evaluate SkillEvolver~\cite{zhang2026skillevolver}, SPARK~\cite{zhou2026spark}, and CoEvoSkills~\cite{zhang2026coevoskills} on SkillsBench~\cite{li2026skillsbench}, with two runs per method. Each run pairs an execution using the generated or evolved skill with a no-skill execution of the same task. Both runs of each method use the same task set: 81 tasks for SkillEvolver, 84 for SPARK, and 85 for CoEvoSkills.
+
+We report mean verifier reward on a 0--100 scale and the mean within-task difference between the skill and no-skill conditions. For a run with $N$ tasks, the paired gain is
+\begin{equation}
+\Delta = \frac{100}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}
+\left(r_i^{\mathrm{skill}}-r_i^{\mathrm{no\text{-}skill}}\right).
+\end{equation}
+We also count tasks with positive, negative, and zero differences.
+
+\begin{table*}[t]
+\centering
+\small
+\setlength{\tabcolsep}{7pt}
+\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrrr}
+\toprule
+\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Run} & \textbf{Tasks} & \textbf{No skill} & \textbf{Skill} & $\boldsymbol{\Delta}$ & \textbf{Improved} & \textbf{Regressed} & \textbf{Tied} \\
+\midrule
+SkillEvolver & 1 & 81 & 30.20 & 55.81 & $+25.61$ & 27 & 6 & 48 \\
+SkillEvolver & 2 & 81 & 37.32 & 62.34 & $+25.02$ & 26 & 3 & 52 \\
+\midrule
+SPARK & 1 & 84 & 17.82 & 19.66 & $+1.84$ & 9 & 8 & 67 \\
+SPARK & 2 & 84 & 14.37 & 20.81 & $+6.45$ & 13 & 5 & 66 \\
+\midrule
+CoEvoSkills & 1 & 85 & 42.21 & 52.44 & $+10.24$ & 17 & 9 & 59 \\
+CoEvoSkills & 2 & 85 & 23.54 & 33.31 & $+9.76$ & 17 & 10 & 58 \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\caption{Two runs per method on SkillsBench. Scores are mean verifier rewards multiplied by 100; $\Delta$ is the mean paired gain in percentage points. Each method uses an identical task set across its two runs.}
+\label{tab:skillsbench-six-runs}
+\end{table*}
+
+\input{skillsbench-variant-table}
+
+\subsection{Do Completed Skills Help on Their Source Tasks?}
+
+All three methods achieve positive mean paired gains in both runs (Table~\ref{tab:skillsbench-six-runs}). SkillEvolver gains 25.61 and 25.02 points, with 27 versus six improved/regressed tasks in run~1 and 26 versus three in run~2. CoEvoSkills gains 10.24 and 9.76 points, with 17 improved tasks in each run and nine versus ten regressions. For these two methods, the paired gains remain similar even though absolute baseline rewards differ between runs.
+
+SPARK has smaller positive gains of 1.84 and 6.45 points. Its first run has nearly balanced improvements and regressions (nine versus eight); the second has 13 improvements and five regressions. Thus, observing a positive mean gain twice can accompany different patterns of task-level benefit.
+
+The repeated positive direction establishes a consistent aggregate pattern across the two runs for each method. The coexistence of improvements, regressions, and ties also shows that skill utility varies across tasks. These paired comparisons establish the observed source-task utility of the completed artifacts. They provide the starting point for the reuse question: whether the content that helps on a source task remains useful when its inputs, instance, or requirements change.
+
+\input{skillsbench-variants}
